[# Section 4 — identity, hashing, binary wire formats.
   Authored 2026-05-22, expanded with cap-bits + ID/IDREF + FFI lifetime.
   Repaired 2026-08-20 against the verification audit — every wire-format
   and canonicalization claim re-sourced from spec/03-approved/core/
   {canonical,cxdm,ast-bin,data-bin,abi}.md and re-recorded against the
   live binary. #]

[section id=identity group='Ring 0 — Data' n=4 title='Identity'

  [intro
    """CX's identity story rests on three layers: **canonical bytes**
       (the unique serialization rule that makes two documents
       comparable), **SHA-256 hashing** (the fingerprint computed over
       those bytes), and the **binary wire formats** (ast-bin for AST
       transfer, data-bin for value transfer). This section is the
       working reference; normative depth in
       [`spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md),
       [`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md) §4,
       [`spec/03-approved/core/ast-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/ast-bin.md), and
       [`spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)."""]

  [child id=canonical n=4.1 title='Canonical bytes'
    [intro
      """Two CX documents are equal iff their **strict canonical bytes**
         are equal. Canonical bytes are the unique serialization
         produced by `cx canonical` — the rules are summarized in
         [[canonical-strict]] and stated normatively in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md)."""]

    [body
      """Why this matters: every distributed-systems primitive in CX —
         caching, deduplication, content-addressing, cross-binding
         equality — depends on byte-stable canonicalization. Without
         it, two "equal" documents might disagree on hash, on equality,
         or on what bytes get shipped over the wire. CX takes the same
         position as JCS for JSON, XCANON for XML, and the Nix store
         for files: there is one true serialization, and the rules to
         produce it are minimal and total."""]

    [child id=canon-lossless-strict n=4.1.1 title='Lossless vs strict canonical (recap)'
      [body
        """Two canonical forms (see [[canonical-strict]] + [[canonical-lossless]]
           in [[surfaces]] for the surface-level treatment):"""]
      [list
        [item """**Lossless canonical** (`cx fmt`) — preserves comments,
                anchors, aliases, presentation. Idempotent. Used for
                source-file formatting and diff stability."""]
        [item """**Strict canonical** (`cx canonical` / `cx hash`) — strips
                comments and directives, expands anchors and merges,
                normalizes datetime offsets to UTC, renames declared ids
                to a deterministic scheme. Two docs with the same data
                hash identically. Used for content addressing."""]]
      [body
        """**The hash is taken over strict canonical bytes**, not over
           lossless. Attribute order is **source order — it is part of
           the data and part of the canonical bytes**. Canonicalization
           never reorders attributes, so `[user email='a' id=1]` and
           `[user id=1 email='a']` are two different documents with two
           different hashes. Order carries meaning in CX; nothing in
           either canonical tier sorts it away."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[; Source (mixed quoting, loose layout): ]
           [user id=42 email='ada@example.com' active=true]

           [; cx canonical output — same attribute order, bare strings
              where the value is bare-eligible: ]
           [user id=42 email=ada@example.com active=true]"""]
      [body
        """**Canonicalization rules (strict):**"""]
      [list
        [item """**Attribute order:** source insertion order, never
                sorted. The only fixed ordering is the element-meta
                slots themselves: anchor (`&`), merge (`*`), id (`#`),
                type annotation, then the attributes."""]
        [item """**String quoting:** bare when the value is
                bare-eligible and would not re-parse as a typed literal;
                single quotes when quoting is required; double quotes
                only to avoid escaping an apostrophe. Escapes are
                minimal — only what the parser requires."""]
        [item """**Whitespace:** no insignificant whitespace; quoted body
                text is preserved verbatim; structural newlines only
                where the layout rules require them."""]
        [item """**Numeric form:** integers as plain decimal digits (hex
                converts to decimal in strict form); floats in the
                shortest representation that round-trips to the same
                value (Ryū shortest-form)."""]
        [item """**Boolean form:** `true` / `false`."""]
        [item """**Date/datetime:** strict canonical **normalizes the
                offset to UTC** (`2026-05-22T10:00:00-04:00` becomes
                `2026-05-22T14:00:00Z`). The lossless form (`cx fmt`)
                preserves the offset as authored."""]
        [item """**Anchors expanded:** `&base` / `*base` structure is
                expanded and removed — the canonical output contains
                only the resolved data, no anchor names."""]
        [item """**Ids renumbered:** `#name` declarations become `id-1`,
                `id-2`, … in document order, and every `@name` reference
                is rewritten to match — so two documents differing only
                in id spelling share one hash."""]]]

    [child id=canon-cross-format n=4.1.2 title='Cross-format identity (hash stable through projections)'
      [body
        """The hash is over CX strict canonical bytes — NOT over the
           projection (JSON / YAML / XML / etc.). This produces a key
           property: **a document's hash is stable through lossless
           round-trip projections**."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """# Original CX
           cx hash users.cx
           # → sha2-256:6c34f4e017fbbb93…

           # Convert to lossless JSON and back
           cx --from=cx --to=json --lossless users.cx > users.json
           cx --from=json --to=cx users.json | cx hash -
           # → sha2-256:6c34f4e017fbbb93…  (same address)"""]
      [body
        """The property holds for the **lossless lanes**: XML, and the
           `--lossless` JSON / YAML envelope encoding (see
           [[roundtrip]]). The **default** (idiomatic) JSON / YAML lanes
           and the TOML lane are typed-lossy — the re-imported document
           is a different document with a different hash. Lossy
           projections (Markdown, CSV/TSV) drop information outright.
           CX makes the lossy-ness explicit; see [[roundtrip]] for the
           full matrix."""]
      [body
        """Practical use: in a content-addressable store, you can
           accept incoming data in a lossless-lane format, convert to
           CX, hash, dedupe — knowing that two clients sending the
           same data through lossless lanes will produce the same key."""]]]

  [child id=hash n=4.2 title='SHA-256 content hash'
    [intro
      """The identity of a CX document is the SHA-256 of its strict
         canonical bytes, reported as a **tagged address** —
         `sha2-256:<64 hex chars>`. The algorithm tag names the default
         of a small crypto-agility registry, so an address is
         self-describing about how it was computed. The hash is
         computed over the UTF-8 byte sequence of the canonical form,
         not over the AST or the in-memory value representation."""]

    [example lang=v
      """// V binding — Layer 1
         doc := cx.parse(source)!
         println(doc.hash())   // 'sha2-256:3a7bd3e2360a...'"""]

    [example lang=python
      """# Python binding — Layer 1
         doc = cx.parse(source)
         print(doc.hash())     # 'sha2-256:3a7bd3e2360a...'"""]

    [example lang=go
      """// Go binding — Layer 1
         doc, _ := cx.Parse(source)
         fmt.Println(doc.Hash())  // "sha2-256:3a7bd3e2360a..."""]

    [example lang=rust
      """// Rust binding — Layer 1
         let doc = cx::parse(&source)?;
         println!("{}", doc.hash());  // "sha2-256:3a7bd3e2360a..."""]

    [child id=hash-stability n=4.2.1 title='Stability across bindings + machines'
      [body
        """The hash function is **stable across bindings, OSes, and
           architectures**. V, Python, Go, and Rust all produce
           byte-identical hash strings for byte-identical input. The
           guarantee extends to architecture (x86-64, ARM64,
           little-endian, big-endian) and OS (macOS, Linux, Windows,
           BSD). Verified by [`conformance/binding_api.cxd`](../../conformance/binding_api.cxd)
           gate 28.6."""]
      [body
        """**Determinism details:**"""]
      [list
        [item """**Float formatting:** the canonical shortest-form
                produces byte-identical output regardless of host C
                library (`printf("%g")` is NOT used; CX uses the Ryū
                shortest round-trip formatter)."""]
        [item """**Endianness:** the canonical bytes are UTF-8 text, no
                multi-byte numeric encoding in the canonical form
                itself."""]
        [item """**Unicode normalization:** identifiers and string
                content are normalized to NFC before canonicalization."""]
        [item """**Locale-independence:** the one place canonical form
                sorts anything — map keys — sorts by the Unicode order
                of the canonical key serialization, never by locale
                collation (no LC_COLLATE influence). Attributes are
                never sorted at all."""]]]

    [child id=hash-truncation n=4.2.2 title='Truncation safety (short ids)'
      [body
        """SHA-256 is 256 bits (64 hex chars). For human-facing
           identifiers you can truncate — a short prefix is usually
           enough to tell a handful of documents apart in a UI, a URL,
           or a log line. The trade-off is the birthday bound: the
           probability of a collision grows with the **square** of the
           collection size, so a prefix that is comfortably unique for
           dozens of items becomes risky for millions. Truncation is a
           display convenience, not an identity: store, compare, and
           sign with the full 64-hex digest, and treat any truncated
           form as resolvable back to it (the way git resolves short
           commit ids)."""]]

    [child id=hash-use-cases n=4.2.3 title='Use cases — cache keys, dedup, content stores'
      [body
        """**Cache key:** `cx hash request.cx` is a stable cache key for
           an expensive computation parameterized on the document.
           Same input → same key → memoization works."""]
      [body
        """**Deduplication:** in a document store, hash on ingestion;
           store the canonical bytes once, point all duplicates at the
           same blob."""]
      [body
        """**Content-addressable storage:** the hash IS the address.
           Git, IPFS, Nix all use the same model. CX hashes interop
           directly:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Git LFS:** point `cx hash`-derived blob ids at the
                CX file's strict-canonical bytes."""]
        [item """**IPFS:** wrap the canonical bytes in a CIDv1 dag-pb
                envelope or use raw codec; the SHA-256 maps to the
                CID's multihash."""]
        [item """**Nix:** identical store-path-shape (SHA-256-named blob
                with metadata sidecar)."""]
        [item """**S3 / object storage:** content-MD5 header is too
                weak; use `x-amz-meta-cx-sha256` for stronger
                integrity."""]]
      [body
        """**Signing:** sign the strict canonical bytes; verify by
           re-canonicalizing on receipt. The signature is robust
           against benign reformatting (whitespace, quoting, comments)
           because those don't affect strict canonical form."""]]]

  [child id=ast-bin n=4.3 title='ast-bin wire format'
    [intro
      """`ast-bin` is the binary serialization of a CX AST. It exists
         for in-process and cross-process transfer of parsed documents
         without re-parsing — bindings ship the AST over the C ABI
         rather than re-tokenizing text. Full spec in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/ast-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/ast-bin.md)."""]

    [body
      """ast-bin is **not** the canonical form. Canonical bytes are CX
         text. ast-bin is the **wire** form between processes that have
         agreed to skip parsing. Two ast-bin payloads can differ
         (different version byte for different feature use) and still
         represent the same canonical document. The hash is always
         taken over canonical text bytes, never over ast-bin."""]

    [child id=ast-bin-envelope n=4.3.1 title='Buffer envelope'
      [body
        """An ast-bin payload is deliberately spare: a 4-byte size
           header, then the payload. There is no magic number, no
           capability-bit block, no varints, and no trailer — feature
           negotiation happens out-of-band via `cx_features` (see
           [[ast-bin-capabilities]]), not inside every buffer. Layout
           per [`spec/03-approved/core/ast-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/ast-bin.md)
           §1–§3:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Size header (4 bytes):** `u32` little-endian byte
                length of the payload (excluding the header itself).
                The buffer is opaque binary, not a null-terminated
                string."""]
        [item """**Version byte (1 byte):** producers emit the *lowest*
                version that carries the document — `6` for the common
                case, `8` when a Path / Match / Modify node is present,
                `9` when an element carries a `[table[…]]` payload.
                Decoders reject buffers versioned above what they
                support."""]
        [item """**Prolog:** `u16` little-endian prolog node count,
                then that many nodes."""]
        [item """**Elements:** `u16` little-endian element count, then
                that many nodes. Each node is `[u8 node-tag]` followed
                by its per-kind payload, recursively — a preorder
                node-tag stream."""]]
      [body
        """Strings on the wire are `[u32 LE byte length][UTF-8 bytes]`
           — fixed-width length prefixes, no null terminators, no
           varints. Optional strings carry a one-byte present/absent
           flag first."""]]

    [child id=ast-bin-capabilities n=4.3.2 title='Capability bits (full table)'
      [body
        """Capability bits are the forward-compat mechanism. `cx_features`
           reports a 64-bit mask of what the loaded libcx implements;
           bindings check it on load and refuse to use features the
           library does not advertise. When a newer libcx adds a
           feature, it claims a new bit; bits are never re-assigned
           (retired bits stay reserved). The full table per
           [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §3:"""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'bit'] [th 'capability']]
        [tr [td '0'] [td 'ABI core conversion symbols (always set)']]
        [tr [td '1'] [td 'Binary AST (cx_to_ast_bin) (always set)']]
        [tr [td '2'] [td 'Binary events (cx_to_events_bin) (always set)']]
        [tr [td '3'] [td 'Symmetric binary AST (round-trip in both directions)']]
        [tr [td '4'] [td 'data-bin core (cx_to_data_bin / cx_from_data_bin)']]
        [tr [td '5'] [td 'data-bin one-shot loaders / dumpers']]
        [tr [td '6'] [td 'Delimited formats (CSV / TSV / PSV / arbitrary single-char)']]
        [tr [td '7'] [td 'Canonical form (cx_fmt / cx_canonical / cx_hash / cx_eq)']]
        [tr [td '8'] [td 'RESERVED — formerly the dedicated CXPath symbol pair; retired (queries route through cx_code_eval)']]
        [tr [td '9'] [td 'Real streaming (cx_events_open / next / close)']]
        [tr [td '10'] [td 'Table block ([table[…]]) in the grammar']]
        [tr [td '11'] [td '::decimal type']]
        [tr [td '12'] [td '::f16 type']]
        [tr [td '13'] [td 'RESERVED — retired boolean-sigil attributes']]
        [tr [td '14'] [td 'Line comments (#)']]
        [tr [td '15'] [td 'Logfmt mode (top-level bare attributes)']]
        [tr [td '16'] [td 'Numeric underscores']]
        [tr [td '17'] [td 'RESERVED — superseded by bit 23']]
        [tr [td '18'] [td 'cx_diff — programmatic semantic diff over strict canonical bytes']]
        [tr [td '19'] [td 'cx_lint — programmatic linting with structured diagnostics']]
        [tr [td '20'] [td 'ID / IDREF C ABI (cx_id_lookup / cx_resolve_ref)']]
        [tr [td '21'] [td 'Chunked-table format (data-bin tag 0x63) + streaming table reader / writer C ABI']]
        [tr [td '22'] [td 'Page-compression wrapper (data-bin tag 0x90, zstd v1)']]
        [tr [td '23'] [td 'Apache Arrow C-Data interop (libcx_arrow)']]
        [tr [td '24'] [td 'Schema-driven data-bin encoding (header flag bit 1)']]
        [tr [td '25'] [td 'Schema validator (cx_validate; RE2-backed [pattern] matching)']]
        [tr [td '26'] [td 'Thread-init handshake (cx_init / cx_thread_register / cx_thread_unregister)']]
        [tr [td '27'] [td 'Streaming-write API (cx_events_writer_*)']]
        [tr [td '28'] [td 'CX code evaluator (cx_code_eval + streaming variant)']]
        [tr [td '29'] [td 'Collection literals — Array / Map / Sequence container kinds end-to-end']]
        [tr [td '30'] [td 'First-class function values ([?fn])']]
        [tr [td '31'] [td 'Program diagram renderer (cx_code_diagram, Mermaid emit)']]
        [tr [td '32'] [td 'CX code tree (cx_code_tree, JSON projection with byte offsets)']]
        [tr [td '33'] [td 'Atom scalar kind (:NAME)']]
        [tr [td '34'] [td '[?def] module-level functions']]
        [tr [td '35'] [td '[?lib] module loading + [?const] + lockfile']]
        [tr [td '36'] [td 'PathNode / MatchNode / ModifyNode wire format (ast-bin v8)']]
        [tr [td '37'] [td 'Iterator wire format (IteratorNode)']]
        [tr [td '38'] [td 'Capability-based security (deny-by-default; [?with-caps])']]
        [tr [td '39'] [td 'Debugging surface (breakpoints, stepping, DAP adapter)']]
        [tr [td '40'] [td 'Element table record wire format (ast-bin v9)']]
        [tr [td '41'] [td 'Runtime-representation engine (demand-driven iterators + full columnar type lattice)']]]
      [body
        """Bits 42–63 are reserved (always zero). The cap-bit layout is
           fixed; bits are never re-assigned — retired features (bits
           8, 13, 17) leave their bits permanently reserved."""]]

    [child id=ast-bin-versioning n=4.3.3 title='Version negotiation + forward-compat'
      [body
        """Negotiation is out-of-band and load-time, not per-buffer.
           `cx_features()` returns a NUL-terminated lowercase **hex
           string** encoding the 64-bit capability mask; a binding
           calls it once on load, caches the mask, and refuses to use
           features the loaded library does not advertise."""]
      [list
        [item """**Producers never lose data silently:** a producer
                asked to encode a value whose kind requires an
                unadvertised bit either implements the kind or refuses
                to encode the document (emitters without the v8 wire
                slot reject Path values with CXER0290). There is no
                downgrade-and-drop path."""]
        [item """**Decoders fail loud:** a buffer whose version byte
                exceeds the decoder's maximum is rejected before any
                node is read, and reserved or unknown node tags are
                rejected rather than skipped-and-guessed."""]
        [item """**Older buffers decode forward:** a newer decoder reads
                any lower-versioned buffer; absent extensions default
                to their zero values."""]]]]

  [child id=data-bin n=4.4 title='data-bin format'
    [intro
      """`data-bin` is the binary value format for **data** — not parse
         structure. Where ast-bin carries AST nodes (with name / kind /
         child pointers), data-bin carries values (scalars, arrays,
         maps, tables) with optional schema-driven tag omission. Spec:
         [`spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)."""]

    [child id=data-bin-self-describing n=4.4.1 title='Self-describing mode'
      [body
        """Every value is tagged with its kind. Comparable to JSON or
           BSON in flexibility. Used when the schema is not known to
           both sides — e.g., wire-formatted between two CX bindings
           that don't share schemas, or for ad-hoc tooling."""]
      [body
        """Tag bytes are one byte, assigned in ranges: `0x00` null,
           `0x01` false, `0x02` true; `0x10`–`0x1F` integers
           (width-tagged — `0x10` int8 through `0x17` uint64, `0x18`
           bigint); `0x20`–`0x2F` floats and decimals; `0x30`–`0x3F`
           string, bytes, and temporal scalars; `0x40`–`0x4F` array
           containers; `0x50`–`0x5F` map containers; `0x60`–`0x6F`
           table containers. Canonical writers select the narrowest
           integer tag that preserves the value. Full tag table in
           [`spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md) §3."""]]

    [child id=data-bin-schema-driven n=4.4.2 title='Schema-driven mode (cap bit 24)'
      [body
        """Per-value type tags are omitted wherever the schema declares
           the value's type; the reader walks the schema in lockstep
           with the data. Wire size comparable to Protobuf or Avro.
           Signaled by capability bit 24 and by **header flag bit 1**
           in the payload itself."""]
      [body
        """**Negotiation:** when flag bit 1 is set, a **schema
           reference** sits in the header, between the fixed 12 bytes
           and the root value — most commonly tag `0x10` followed by
           32 bytes: the SHA-256 of the schema's strict canonical text.
           (An inline-schema form and a hash-plus-name-hint form also
           exist.) Readers resolve the schema by content hash and fail
           closed if it is unavailable or its hash mismatches — never
           silently corrupted bytes. Tag omission is per-field:
           declared fields drop their tags, undeclared fields fall back
           to self-describing form, so both modes coexist in one
           document."""]]

    [child id=data-bin-chunked n=4.4.3 title='Chunked tables (§3.11)'
      [body
        """Tables that exceed memory get a dedicated wire form —
           the chunked table, tag `0x63` per
           [`spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
           §3.11. Layout:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Column specs, once:** column count, then per-column
                name + type — the same col-spec shape as the in-memory
                table form."""]
        [item """**Row groups:** zero or more, each
                `uvarint(body-byte-length)` + a one-byte body tag
                (`0x01` plain, `0x90` zstd-compressed) + the body —
                row count plus column-major payloads for that group's
                rows."""]
        [item """**End marker:** a single `0x00` closes the table."""]]
      [body
        """The byte-length prefix on every row group is the whole
           skip-ahead mechanism: a reader can jump group to group
           without decoding bodies it doesn't need, and memory is
           bounded by the largest single row group. There are no
           per-chunk statistics, no manifest, and no trailer —
           predicate pushdown is deliberately ceded to Parquet via the
           Arrow bridge (see [[analytics]])."""]]

    [child id=data-bin-compression n=4.4.4 title='Page-compression wrapper (§3.12)'
      [body
        """A row-group body may be wrapped in compression (the `0x90`
           body tag, per
           [`spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
           §3.12; capability bit 22). Exactly one codec is defined:
           **zstd v1** (codec-id `0x01`); readers reject any other
           codec id with error D005. The wrapper applies only at the
           row-group body position — never at value level or inside a
           column payload."""]
      [body
        """The hash is taken over the **uncompressed** bytes:
           compression level, dictionary use, and zstd block boundaries
           do not affect `cx_hash`. Storage/transport concerns and
           content identity stay orthogonal."""]]]

  [child id=id-anchors n=4.5 title='ID anchors and IDREF resolution'
    [intro
      """The `#name` sigil (see [[id]]) declares a semantic identity on
         an element; the `@name` sigil references it. Identities are
         document-scoped. Normative spec:
         [`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md) §4."""]

    [child id=id-scoping n=4.5.1 title='Document-unique scoping'
      [body
        """An identity `#name` is unique within a single CX document.
           A duplicate `#name` declaration is refused when the document
           is read, with `CXER0208` (E_DUPLICATE_ID) — the error names
           the id and the fact that it is declared on more than one
           element."""]
      [body
        """Identities do NOT cross document boundaries. Two separate CX
           files can each declare `#ada` without conflict; they refer
           to different elements in their respective documents."""]]

    [child id=idref-resolution n=4.5.2 title='IDREF resolution + cycles'
      [body
        """A reference to an identity uses the `@name` sigil in
           **attribute-value position**: `[post author-id=@ada …]`.
           In body position, the reference form is a `[ref @name]`
           element. `#` declares; `@` references — the two sigils are
           never interchangeable."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[users
           [user #ada email='ada@example.com']
           [user #grace email='grace@example.com']]
         [posts
           [post author-id=@ada 'First post']
           [post author-id=@grace 'Second']]"""]
      [body
        """**Reference cycles** (A references B references A) are not
           an error — the references are values, not container
           relationships; no infinite recursion. However, cyclic
           **merge** references (via the `*` sigil — see [[merge]])
           ARE errors: a document whose anchors resolve through
           themselves has no finite expanded form, so canonicalization
           refuses it outright."""]
      [body
        """The ID/IDREF model is XML-compatible: round-trip through
           XML preserves the references (`xml:id` + IDREF-typed
           attributes). The semantic JSON / YAML / TOML lanes emit an
           ordinary `id` field and a `{"$ref": "…"}` envelope — a
           one-way, human-readable projection per
           [`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md)
           §1.1."""]]

    [child id=id-cross-format n=4.5.3 title='Identity preservation across projections'
      [body
        """Only CX and XML carry the ID/IDREF mechanism losslessly;
           the other lanes flatten it into plain fields
           ([`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §1.1):"""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'format'] [th 'ID encoding'] [th 'IDREF encoding'] [th 'round-trip']]
        [tr [td 'CX (canonical)'] [td '#name on the element'] [td '@name in value position; [ref @name] in body'] [td 'lossless']]
        [tr [td 'XML'] [td 'xml:id="name"'] [td 'IDREF-typed attribute value'] [td 'lossless']]
        [tr [td 'JSON / YAML / TOML / MD (semantic)'] [td 'ordinary "id" field'] [td '{"$ref": "name"} envelope'] [td 'one-way — the import direction does not reconstruct @-references']]]
      [body
        """The `$ref` shape is operator-facing (humans recognize it as
           a reference); it is not machine-round-trip-authoritative.
           When identity must survive a pipeline, route it through CX
           or XML."""]]]

  [child id=ffi-lifetime n=4.6 title='FFI lifetime and memory ownership'
    [intro
      """Every binding wraps the libcx C ABI. A Python `Doc` (or Go,
         Rust, V Doc) is a host-language value. **Who owns the memory?
         When does it get freed?** This section is the developer-facing
         answer; the normative contract is
         [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §1.4–§1.5."""]

    [child id=ffi-doc-lifetime n=4.6.1 title='Ownership across the C ABI'
      [body
        """The ABI is **string-in / string-out**: a conversion symbol
           takes a UTF-8 buffer, does its work, and returns a
           freshly-allocated buffer. There is no persistent document
           handle living inside libcx — no reference to release, no
           finalizer to register for ordinary conversions."""]
      [list
        [item """**Returned buffers are caller-owned:** every string or
                byte buffer libcx returns is released with `cx_free` —
                never with the system `free()`."""]
        [item """**A binding's `Doc` is host-owned:** it holds the
                document in the host language's own memory (text and/or
                a binding-native AST decoded from ast-bin). Its
                lifetime is ordinary host memory management — V and Go
                garbage-collect it, Python reference-counts it, Rust
                drops it at end of scope. Nothing in libcx needs to be
                notified."""]
        [item """**The exceptions are the streaming handles:** the
                event reader (`cx_events_open` / `cx_events_next` /
                `cx_events_close`) and the streaming table reader /
                writer keep state across calls behind an opaque handle
                with an explicit close. Bindings wrap these in the
                host's resource idiom (context manager, defer,
                Drop)."""]]
      [body
        """**Practical implications:**"""]
      [list
        [item """**Long-lived Docs:** cost host memory only; libcx
                holds nothing on their behalf between calls."""]
        [item """**Multi-process sharing:** ast-bin is the wire format
                — serialize on one side, deserialize on the other.
                There is no shared-memory Doc passing."""]]]

    [child id=ffi-node-borrowing n=4.6.2 title='Nodes are owned values, not borrows'
      [body
        """A `Node` selected from a `Doc` (via `select_all`, `select`,
           `find_all`, etc.) is an **owned host value** — a
           binding-native piece of tree, not a pointer into libcx
           memory. Nothing is borrowed across the FFI boundary, so
           there is no lifetime coupling to manage:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Dropping the Doc does not invalidate Nodes** already
                selected from it — each Node stands on its own in host
                memory."""]
        [item """**No cross-language GC interplay:** the host language
                manages Docs and Nodes exactly like any other value it
                allocated itself."""]]
      [body
        """**Doc.bytes() output is owned:** the canonical bytes
           returned from `doc.bytes()` are a freshly-allocated copy
           owned by the caller. Modifying or freeing it does not
           affect the Doc."""]
      [body
        """**Doc.modify returns a NEW Doc:** the input Doc is
           unchanged — value semantics, not aliasing. The two Docs
           lead independent lives; releasing one never affects the
           other."""]]

    [child id=ffi-thread-register n=4.6.3 title='Thread registration (cx_thread_register)'
      [body
        """libcx uses Boehm GC, which requires that every thread that
           calls libcx APIs is **registered** with the GC. The C ABI
           exports `cx_init()` (call once per process at start) and
           `cx_thread_register()` (call per thread before first
           libcx call from that thread). Per
           [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §1.5."""]
      [body
        """**Bindings handle this automatically** at the FFI chokepoint
           — every Layer-1 method has a per-thread guard that
           registers the calling thread on first use. Host application
           code doesn't call `cx_thread_register` directly unless
           dropping below the binding layer to raw FFI."""]
      [body
        """**Cap bit 26** advertises the thread-init handshake. It is
           required for bindings whose host runtime spawns OS threads
           outside V's control (Rust, C#, Java), and cheap and harmless
           everywhere else."""]
      [body
        """**Practical implication:** spawning a thread from host code
           and calling a CX binding method from it is safe — the
           binding registers the thread transparently. The only
           caveat: if you're calling libcx via raw FFI (not through a
           binding), you must call `cx_thread_register()` yourself
           before any other libcx call from that thread."""]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/concepts/identity.cx,
     docs-src/content/reference/ast-bin.cx, and
     docs-src/content/reference/data-bin.cx during Phase B docs →
     guide consolidation (2026-05-24). Higher-level framing on
     two-layer identity (content-addressed + declarational) and
     compact quick-references for ast-bin / data-bin. #]

  [child id=identity-framing n=4.7 title='Identity framing — two layers + binary forms'
    [intro
      """Identity in CX has two layers. The first is
         content-addressed — every tree has a SHA-256 over its
         canonical bytes; two trees with the same data have the
         same hash. The second is declarational — an element can
         carry an id via `#`, and other places in the document can
         reference it via `@`."""]

    [child id=canonical-recap n=4.7.1 title='Canonical form recap'
      [body
        """Canonical mode strips presentation — comments, anchors,
           whitespace — normalizes datetimes to UTC, renames ids to
           their deterministic document-order names, and emits a
           data-equivalent byte-identical form. Two documents with
           the same data emit to the same bytes; the difference
           between them is something a human added, not something
           the data carries. (Attribute order is data, not
           presentation — it is preserved and hashed.)"""]
      [example lang=shell
        """cx canonical menu.cx     # canonical bytes
           cx hash menu.cx          # sha2-256:<hex> over canonical bytes
           cx eq a.cx b.cx          # exit 0 iff canonical(a) == canonical(b)"""]
      [note
        """Canonical form is the basis for content-addressed
           identity (`cx hash` — a tagged address, `sha2-256:` being
           the registry default), for byte-identical conformance
           tests across bindings, and for diff that ignores
           presentation."""]]

    [child id=declared-ids n=4.7.2 title='Declared identifiers (#id, @ref)'
      [body
        """Declared identity is what you write when you want one
           element to point at another by name. Declare with `#`;
           reference with `@`. IDs are unique within their scope;
           the scope is the document root by default."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[order #main
             [item #latte]
             [item #scone]]
           [receipt order=@main]"""]]

    [child id=anchors-merge n=4.7.3 title='Anchors and merge targets'
      [body
        """An anchor (`&` sigil) names a value once and lets later
           places refer to it without repeating it. A merge target
           (`*` sigil) folds the anchor referent into the current
           element. Anchors are anonymous handles; the strict
           canonical form **expands every merge and removes the
           anchor structure entirely** — no anchor name survives
           into the canonical bytes, so two documents that bind the
           same data with different anchor names hash to the same
           identity."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[defaults &shared timeout=30 retries=3]
           [server *shared name=api]
           [server *shared name=worker]"""]]

    [child id=ast-bin-quickref n=4.7.4 title='ast-bin quick reference'
      [body
        """ast-bin is the binary serialization of the canonical
           form. The buffer envelope is `[u32 LE size][payload
           bytes]`; payload is the AST as a preorder node-tag
           stream whose node kinds are gated by capability bits
           (column-major layout belongs to data-bin's table
           payloads, not to ast-bin). Release the buffer with
           `cx_free`; never call system `free()`. Capability bit
           36 advertises the PathNode wire format (ast_bin v8);
           cap bit 3 advertises symmetric AST
           round-trip. The full bit table is in
           [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md)
           §3."""]]

    [child id=data-bin-quickref n=4.7.5 title='data-bin quick reference (CXCol v1)'
      [body
        """data-bin is the strict-canonical binary data format —
           format identifier **CXCol v1**. The magic is the five
           ASCII bytes `CXCol` (`0x43 0x58 0x43 0x6F 0x6C`);
           readers reject anything else outright. Modes:
           self-describing (default), schema-driven (cap bit 24 —
           header flag bit 1, schema referenced by the SHA-256 of
           its strict canonical text), chunked tables
           ([`spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
           §3.11, cap bit 21), and page-compressed (zstd v1, cap
           bit 22). Chunk boundaries are part of the canonical
           bytes, so chunked output is byte-stable across writers
           only at the canonical chunk size (2²⁰ rows per group);
           compressed row groups hash over their uncompressed
           bytes, so compression level never moves an address."""]]]
]
